## 00906 PATIENT=1 DOCTOR=2
1: I get headaches in the afternoon, and I wonder if it's my eyes. ~ cc s1
2: Good question. Where do you feel the headache? ~ s1
1: Across my forehead, and sometimes behind the eyes. ~ s1
2: How long have you had them? ~ s1
1: About three weeks. Since I started the new job at the computer. ~ s1
2: How many hours a day are you looking at a screen? ~
1: Eight or nine. Sometimes more. ~
2: Do you notice blurred vision, or double vision? ~ s2
1: Blurred, towards the end of the day. Words go a bit fuzzy. ~ s2
2: Any flashing lights, or vomiting with the headache? ~ n1
1: No. Nothing like that. ~ n1
2: Do you take anything for the headache? ~ m1
1: Ibuprofen, 400 mg, when it's bad. Maybe twice a week. ~ m1
2: And have you ever had your eyes tested?
1: Not since school. I had glasses then, but I lost them.
2: Any allergies I should know about, to medicines? ~ a1
1: No, none that I know of. ~ a1
2: Any long-term conditions, or family history of eye problems? ~ h2
1: My mother has glaucoma. ~ h2
2: That's important, thank you. I'll check your blood pressure and pulse. ~
1: Okay. ~ pause=10
2: Blood pressure 118 over 76, pulse 68, temperature 36.5, oxygen 99 percent, breathing 14. ~ v
1: Is it all right? ~
2: Perfect. I think this is eye strain, from the screen time and probably needing glasses again. ~ d1
1: That's a relief. I was worried it was something worse. ~
2: With your mother's glaucoma, I'd like an optician to measure the pressure in your eyes too. ~ p1
1: Can I book it myself? ~
2: Yes, a full eye test, and tell them about your mother. ~
1: I'll do it this week. ~
2: Meanwhile, take a break every twenty minutes and look at something far away. ~ c1
1: The twenty-twenty rule, I've heard of it. ~
2: Exactly. And adjust the screen so it isn't glaring. ~ c2
1: I'll move my desk away from the window. ~
2: If you get sudden loss of vision, eye pain, or vomiting with the headache, go to the emergency department. ~ c3
1: Understood. Thanks, doctor. ~
@@
